### What is the Top K Frequent Numbers problem?

Given an unsorted array of numbers, find the K numbers that appear most frequently. If two numbers have the same frequency, either can be chosen. This combines frequency counting with the Top K Elements pattern.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

public class TopKFrequent {

    public static List<Integer> findTopKFrequent(int[] categories, int k) {
        // Step 1: Count frequencies
        Map<Integer, Integer> freqMap = new HashMap<>();
        for (int cat : categories) {
            freqMap.merge(cat, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        // Step 2: Min-heap of size K keyed on frequency
        PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> freqMap.get(a) - freqMap.get(b)
        );

        for (int key : freqMap.keySet()) {
            minHeap.offer(key);
            if (minHeap.size() > k) {
                minHeap.poll(); // evict least frequent
            }
        }

        return new ArrayList<>(minHeap);
    }
}
```

### Why use a min-heap keyed on frequency?

The min-heap root holds the number with the lowest frequency among the K numbers currently in the heap. When a new number is offered and the heap size exceeds K, polling the root evicts the least frequent number, exactly the weakest candidate. This mirrors the Top K Largest pattern but with frequency as the comparison key instead of the value itself.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

public class FrequencyHeapDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        // Categories: [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4]
        // Frequencies: {1:3, 2:2, 3:4, 4:1}
        // K=2

        // Offer 1 (freq=3): heap = [1]              (size 1)
        // Offer 2 (freq=2): heap = [2, 1]            (size 2, root=2 freq=2)
        // Offer 3 (freq=4): heap = [2, 1, 3]         (size 3 > K=2)
        //   Poll: evict 2 (freq=2, least frequent)
        //   heap = [1, 3]                             (root=1 freq=3)
        // Offer 4 (freq=1): heap = [4, 1, 3]         (size 3 > K=2)
        //   Poll: evict 4 (freq=1, least frequent)
        //   heap = [1, 3]                             (root=1 freq=3)
        // Final: top 2 frequent = [1 (freq 3), 3 (freq 4)]
    }
}
```

### How does frequency counting work?

A single pass over the array with `map.merge(num, 1, Integer::sum)` counts occurrences. This is O(n) time and O(d) space where d is the number of distinct values. The merge method is cleaner than the get-or-default-plus-put pattern.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FrequencyCount {

    public static void count() {
        int[] transactions = {101, 202, 101, 303, 202, 101, 303, 303, 303};
        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (int txn : transactions) {
            freq.merge(txn, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        System.out.println(freq); // {101=3, 202=2, 303=4}
    }
}
```

