### What is the bucket sort alternative?

When K is close to the number of distinct values, a bucket sort by frequency gives O(n) time. Create an array of buckets indexed by frequency (0 to n), place each number in its frequency bucket, then read from the highest bucket downward until K numbers are collected.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class BucketSortFrequency {

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public static List<Integer> findTopKBucket(int[] nums, int k) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (int num : nums) freq.merge(num, 1, Integer::sum);

        List<Integer>[] buckets = new List[nums.length + 1];
        for (var entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            int f = entry.getValue();
            if (buckets[f] == null) buckets[f] = new ArrayList<>();
            buckets[f].add(entry.getKey());
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = buckets.length - 1; i >= 0 && result.size() < k; i--) {
            if (buckets[i] != null) result.addAll(buckets[i]);
        }
        return result.subList(0, Math.min(k, result.size()));
    }
}
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Approach | Time | Space | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|
| Frequency map + min-heap | O(n + d log K) | O(n + K) | K much smaller than d |
| Frequency map + bucket sort | O(n) | O(n) | K close to d |
| Sort by frequency | O(n + d log d) | O(n) | When simplicity matters |

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include K equal to zero (return empty), K equal to the number of distinct values (return all distinct values), all elements being the same (that one element is the only answer for K=1), and ties in frequency.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

import java.util.List;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        System.out.println(TopKFrequent.findTopKFrequent(new int[]{1,1,2,2,3}, 0)); // []
        System.out.println(TopKFrequent.findTopKFrequent(new int[]{5,5,5}, 1));     // [5]
        // Ties: {1:2, 2:2, 3:1}, K=1 -> either 1 or 2 is valid
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is ordering the heap by the number's value instead of its frequency. Another mistake is iterating over the original array instead of the frequency map's key set, which would offer duplicate entries to the heap.

```java
package com.bank.analytics;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: heap ordered by value, not frequency
    // PriorityQueue<Integer> heap = new PriorityQueue<>();  // orders by value!
    // Fix: use comparator that compares freqMap.get(a) vs freqMap.get(b)

    // WRONG: iterating over original array instead of distinct keys
    // for (int num : nums) heap.offer(num);  // offers duplicates!
    // Fix: iterate over freqMap.keySet()
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should mention both the heap and bucket sort approaches and explain when each is better. The bucket sort approach is O(n) but uses more space for the bucket array. For streaming data where new numbers keep arriving, the heap approach is better because it can process incrementally. In a distributed system, each node computes local frequency maps and top-K heaps, and the coordinator merges them.

```java
// Senior considerations:
// 1. Heap: O(n + d log K), better when K << d
// 2. Bucket sort: O(n), better when K close to d
// 3. Streaming: heap works incrementally
// 4. Distributed: local freq maps + local heaps, merge at coordinator
// 5. Approximate: count-min sketch for very large streams
```
